      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level effect: anchor on August's original print. Momentum effect: partially carry forward the recent decline. September payroll evidence supports a subdued demand interpretation, but supplies no calibrated numerical mapping to vacancies, so it adds no separate adjustment. One-off effect: do not extrapolate April's concentrated business-services surge; retain it in historical volatility. Policy-mechanism effect: no independent adjustment because the fetched evidence establishes no quantified September vacancy effect from a policy action."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use an August persistence prior with judgmental 0.5 shrinkage of recent momentum. The January-August first-print sample gives seven successive changes in millions: [-0.064, -0.016, +0.752, -0.024, -0.235, -0.088, -0.192]. Their mean is 0.019 and squared deviations sum to 0.667738; sigma = 0.333601 million = sqrt(0.667738 / 6). Recent-three mean change = (-0.235 - 0.088 - 0.192) / 3 = -0.171667. Point = 7.079 + 0.5 \u00d7 (-0.171667) = 6.993167; separate level-reversion, one-off, payroll, and policy adjustments are zero. The central normal 80% interval uses half-width = 1.28 \u00d7 sigma = 0.427009, without additional scaling. Implied bounds are 6.566157 and 7.420176 million; rounded point and bounds are 6.993, 6.566, and 7.420."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The 0.5 momentum weight is a forecasting judgment, not an estimated coefficient. Successive original prints contain revision noise as well as economic movement. Seven changes provide a small dispersion sample, and April's large increase materially affects sigma; the 80% coverage is a model assumption rather than demonstrated calibration."
